      "id": 1,
      "text": "Edenton was incorporated in 1722 and named for Governor Charles Eden, who died that same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edenton%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Cupola House, built in 1758 by Francis Corbin, is a National Historic Landmark that still stands on the waterfront.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edenton%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1767 Chowan County Courthouse, another National Historic Landmark, has been in continuous use since its construction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edenton%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Edenton was once North Carolina’s second‑oldest town and a major port that helped enslaved people escape via the Maritime Underground Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://historicsites.nc.gov/all-sites/historic-edenton",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits at the north end of Edenton Bay, where the Chowan and Roanoke rivers meet to form Albemarle Sound.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edenton%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1774, Edenton hosted the first political action by women in the colonies, led by Penelope Barker.",
